  "content": {
    "id": "p27-q4-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "4",
    "prompt": "Initially there are 20 grams of undissolved salt in water. After 3 minutes, there are 10 grams of undissolved salt. Assuming the mass of undissolved salt is decaying exponentially, determine how long it will take for there to be 1 gram of undissolved salt, correct to the nearest minute.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "13 minutes.",
      "worked": "Let $M(t)$ be the mass of undissolved salt in grams after $t$ minutes. The mass halves every 3 minutes, so\n$$M(t)=20\\left(\\frac12\\right)^{t/3}.$$\nSetting $M(t)=1$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\n20\\left(\\frac12\\right)^{t/3}&=1\\\\\n\\left(\\frac12\\right)^{t/3}&=\\frac1{20}\\\\\n\\log_{10}\\left[\\left(\\frac12\\right)^{t/3}\\right]&=\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac1{20}\\right)\\\\\n\\frac{t}{3}\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac12\\right)&=\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac1{20}\\right)\\\\\nt&=\\frac{3\\log_{10}(1/20)}{\\log_{10}(1/2)}\\\\\nt&\\approx12.9658.\n\\end{align*}$$\nIt will take approximately 13 minutes.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 70
  },
